## Math (KaTeX)

Inline: $E = mc^2$ appears mid-sentence, and prices like $5 and $10 stay literal.

$$
\int_0^\infty e^{-x^2}\,dx = \frac{\sqrt{\pi}}{2}
$$

## Diagrams (Mermaid)

```mermaid
graph TD
    A[Start] --> B{Decision}
    B -->|Yes| C[Continue]
    B -->|No| D[Stop]
```

## Cross-file links and embeds

Links and embeds use the `lorekeep://file/<id>` scheme and are written by the link toolbar, not by hand. Use the toolbar to add a link to another file, or embed a Diagram or Sheet chart, e.g. `![Trade routes](lorekeep://file/<id>)`.

## Standard Markdown still works

| Feature | Supported |
|---|---|
| Tables | yes |
| ~~Strikethrough~~ | yes |
| `Inline code` | yes |

> Blockquotes, **bold**, *italic*, and [links](https://example.com) are unchanged.

\\\

# PDF output

This heading starts on a new page, because every single `#` heading does. The line before it holds three backslashes, which is a page break that shows nothing in the editor. `[PAGEBREAK]` does the same but leaves a dashed rule in the editor.

Exporting to PDF opens a dialog for paper, margins, page numbers, header and footer. Its defaults come from the `[PAGE ...]` line at the top of this file, which never prints:

| Option | Example |
|---|---|
| Paper | `size=A4` or `landscape` |
| Margins | `margin=2cm` or `margin="2.5cm 2cm"` |
| Header and footer | `header="My book" footer="Page {page} of {pages}"` |
| Page numbers | `numbers=bottom-right` |
| Plain first page | `first-page=plain` |
| Body size and spacing | `fontsize=12 spacing=1.3 paragraph=0.8em` |
| Paragraph indent | `indent=1.5em` |
| Which heading breaks pages | `newpage=h2` or `newpage=none` |
| "Chapter N" above each `#` | `chapter-label="Chapter"` |
| Heading styles | `h1-size=Huge h1-center h1-bold` |
| Hide the file-name title | `title=off` |

Text is never cut at the bottom of a page: paragraphs and tables carry on at the next page, splitting only between lines or rows.
